<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Passion of Jesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 30–33 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Passion of Christ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Passion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is the Christian term for the suffering of Jesus in the last hours of his life, from his arrest in the garden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gethsemane">Gethsemane</a> through his trial before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate">Pontius Pilate</a> to his crucifixion outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> around 30 to 33 CE. It is told in all four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and is central to Christian worship, art and devotion, including the veneration of relics believed to come from it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/passion-of-jesus.jpg" alt="Passion of Jesus" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Stom · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matthias_Stom_-_Christ_Crowned_with_Thorns.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Passion, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> patior, to suffer, is described in the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a>, Matthew, Mark, Luke and John. The first three, the Synoptic Gospels, give similar accounts, while John's varies significantly. Scholars do not agree which events belong to the Passion narrative. Some begin it after the arrest and before the resurrection, so that it holds only the trials, crucifixion and death. Others take a broader approach that includes the triumphal entry, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a>, the burial and the resurrection.</p><p>Christians interpret passages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> as prophecies of the Passion, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 52:13 to 53:12, which describes a sinless man who atones for the sins of his people by voluntary suffering. In the Gospel of Mark Jesus predicts his own Passion and resurrection three times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A comprehensive account includes his triumphal entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, the cleansing of the Temple, where he expels livestock merchants and money-changers, his anointing by a woman a few days before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a>, where he predicts his betrayal. On the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gethsemane">Gethsemane</a> he predicts that Peter will deny him three times. The gospels give differing accounts of his trial. Mark describes two proceedings, one before Jewish leaders and one before the Roman prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate">Pontius Pilate</a>, and Matthew and John generally follow him. Luke alone adds a third, in which Pilate sends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a>, who mocks him and returns him to Pilate.</p><p>All the gospels relate that Pilate released a man named Barabbas instead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Matthew, Mark and John have Pilate offer the crowd a choice, whereas Luke has the crowd demand Barabbas's release. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Matthew call him Jesus Barabbas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Passion is commemorated each year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> during Holy Week. Most denominations read a narrative of it, especially on Good Friday, and in the Roman Rite whole congregations take part in reading the Passion Gospel on Palm Sunday and Good Friday. The Stations of the Cross, a series of reflections on Christ carrying the cross, began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi">Francis of Assisi</a> and spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>.</p><p>Each episode, such as the Flagellation or Entombment, has been shown thousands of times in art, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a> most of all, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer">Albrecht Dürer</a> produced three versions of his Passion print cycle. Passion plays are performed in many Catholic countries, notably at Oberammergau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Passion of Jesus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Relics presented to the faithful included textiles believed to be from the <b>Passion of Christ</b>, shown every seven years in the Aachen pilgrimage.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-kornelimunster">Imperial Abbey of Kornelimünster</a></li></ul></section>
